11
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:03.110
Someone says they can't hear any sound.

12
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:05.770
It would be helpful

13
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.290
if you could confirm whether you can hear us.

14
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.250
Otherwise, it may be a problem at your end.

15
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:20.730
I can hear you, Fredrik, so I
assume it's working for everyone.

16
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.250
Yes, and I can hear you clearly.

17
00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.790
Daniel says he can hear us.

18
00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.109
There's a new feature on this new platform.

19
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:07.510
I can reply privately to
people who submit questions.

20
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:08.710
I think it's private.

21
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.310
But you can hear us, so let's carry on.

22
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.989
Let's start with Börstjänarfolket.

23
00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:21.650
We have some encouraging figures.

24
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.719
It remains strongly correlated with crypto.

25
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.020
Crypto has had a strong rally,

26
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:39.680
which lifted Börstjänarfolket
by almost 21% in September.

27
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:49.040
Bittensor remains the main
driver of that portfolio.

28
00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.910
The start of trading is
approaching for Babylon Capital.

29
00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.830
Where do we stand, Fredrik?

30
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.720
Yes, I think it's just as you've written here.

31
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.580
The last capital is coming in.

32
00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.820
One investor was a little late
transferring their capital,

33
00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:21.100
so we're tidying up the final details.

34
00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:29.020
As you say, we've finally
received Nordea's approval.

35
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.760
So we're on track.

36
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.140
We've arranged our dollar and euro accounts.

37
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:43.720
There's also one small
step remaining with StoneX.

38
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.799
There have been 10 to 15
rounds of correspondence.

39
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.560
They've asked questions and
requested further documentation.

40
00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.760
But they now seem very
close to being satisfied.

41
00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.760
Yes, there have been quite a few questions.

42
00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:10.160
We'll explain who StoneX
is later this evening.

43
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.020
It's our FCM.

44
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.040
We'll also explain what that means.

45
00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.880
We've received a question by email.

46
00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:28.040
How will my Babylon investment appear when
it's transferred to my securities account?

47
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.560
And will that happen during October?

48
00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.020
Yes, I think it should.

49
00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.319
But I can't guarantee it.

50
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:51.299
There are a few steps left before

51
00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.440
everything is ready at Kaptena.

52
00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.980
There's also a final approval from the
Swedish Companies Registration Office.

53
00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:04.040
So I think it should be
ready by the end of October.

54
00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.559
But we can't give a firm guarantee.

55
00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.020
We don't need to wait for
that to begin trading.

56
00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.950
Then there's the next opportunity to invest.

57
00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.770
We mentioned this briefly last month.

58
00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:30.130
I'm not sure yet whether
it will be November 1.

59
00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:32.700
But I know some people are interested in

60
00:05:32.701 --> 00:05:37.929
increasing their
investment in Babylon Capital.

61
00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.730
There will be opportunities to do that.

62
00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:52.510
If you'd like to increase your
investment, please contact us now,

63
00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.510
and we'll put you on the list.

64
00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.990
Existing investors can add to their
holdings, and new investors can also join.

65
00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.700
Now...

66
00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:20.590
we come to this evening's lesson.

67
00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:28.750
Last time, we said we'd help you, as
investors, learn about Managed Futures

68
00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.810
and this type of investment.

69
00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:41.567
You need to understand how the investment

70
00:06:41.568 --> 00:06:46.010
may fluctuate and what to expect.

71
00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:51.650
But it's also quite important to
understand how everything fits together.

72
00:06:52.370 --> 00:07:00.550
The structure may seem

73
00:07:00.551 --> 00:07:02.340
a little complicated.

74
00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.100
In fact, it's all very straightforward.

75
00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:15.740
Babylon Capital is a Swedish limited
company in which you buy shares.

76
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.881
It operates as a commodity
pool, with investors

77
00:07:21.882 --> 00:07:28.700
owning shares in the company.

78
00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:37.740
The capital belongs to the company.

79
00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.589
Babylon Capital then engages

80
00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:51.869
the Hallenborg CTA program,

81
00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:56.700
which handles the strategy,
risk and trading decisions.

82
00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:03.890
I'm the person who runs this CTA program.

83
00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.270
We're assisted by GFF Brokers.

84
00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:20.750
GFF Brokers is a smaller
company in the United States.

85
00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:28.770
Their size works to our advantage,
because we receive help and support.

86
00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.010
They're very committed to serving us.

87
00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.810
I've worked with GFF

88
00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.669
since I opened my account in, I think, 2001,

89
00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:51.530
before the current owners owned or
even worked at the company.

90
00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.470
We have an excellent relationship with them.

91
00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:03.819
They manage the relationship
with StoneX, our FCM.

92
00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:10.600
That's where clearing, margin and the
protection of customer funds are handled.

93
00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:14.159
StoneX looks after the capital.

94
00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:22.539
Think of GFF Brokers as providing

95
00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:27.386
the personal service that larger

96
00:09:27.387 --> 00:09:32.940
firms can lose because of their size.

97
00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:40.620
StoneX is a Fortune 500 company
and a very large organisation.

98
00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:46.540
GFF Brokers helps us keep
everything running smoothly.

99
00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:55.620
Someone answers the phone when we
call, and our business matters to them.

100
00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:02.620
We access the futures markets through StoneX.

101
00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:08.753
This is electronic today.

102
00:10:08.754 --> 00:10:15.740
All trading takes place on
platforms that handle our orders.

103
00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.360
We no longer need to phone in orders

104
00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:25.420
for a runner on the floor to
deliver to a trader.

105
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.620
That was the old system.
Today it's all electronic.

106
00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.680
That change came after COVID.

107
00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:40.659
They closed the floor during
COVID and traded electronically,

108
00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.220
and the floor never reopened.

109
00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.964
A very important point is

110
00:10:49.965 --> 00:10:57.080
that the capital belongs to Babylon Capital.

111
00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.400
As the CTA, we don't control the capital.

112
00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:07.820
GFF doesn't control it either.

113
00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:12.579
As we'll see shortly,
StoneX doesn't have direct

114
00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:20.680
control over the capital either.

115
00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:26.460
The capital is held at StoneX in a segregated
account, which we'll come to shortly.

116
00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:36.700
Put simply, I'm responsible
for managing the investments.

117
00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:45.980
I do that under a mandate to
manage Babylon's account.

118
00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:55.429
My role has nothing to
do with holding the capital.

119
00:11:55.430 --> 00:12:00.270
I don't hold it in my own
name, and I can't make withdrawals.

120
00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:05.590
I can't touch the capital.
It belongs to Babylon.

121
00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:13.700
GFF and StoneX provide the infrastructure.

122
00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:26.929
StoneX is our FCM.

123
00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:30.930
FCM stands for Futures Commission Merchant.

124
00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:47.070
This is where everything
happens in futures trading.

125
00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:55.250
They hold all the customer
accounts and adjust the margin.

126
00:12:56.390 --> 00:13:01.250
Margin is the capital you hold in the account.

127
00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:10.970
A futures contract is an agreement for
future delivery, with the price agreed today.

128
00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:20.510
You might agree to buy $100,000
worth of crude oil in a year's time.

129
00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.350
But you don't need to put up $100,000 today.

130
00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:28.790
You put up margin, which
varies between markets.

131
00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.890
It might be 10%.

132
00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:39.970
Then you only need $10,000 to
control a $100,000 position.

133
00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:44.271
When delivery is due, you need

134
00:13:44.272 --> 00:13:49.789
the full $100,000 to pay for it.

135
00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:53.630
But as speculators, we never take it that far.

136
00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:59.708
We trade futures for speculative
purposes, seeking to benefit

137
00:13:59.709 --> 00:14:06.790
from market volatility and the
tendency to trend up or down.

138
00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:15.390
All positions are closed before
physical delivery is due.

139
00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:24.325
Clearing is another important
part: buy orders are matched

140
00:14:24.326 --> 00:14:31.709
with sell orders, and someone
guarantees the system.

141
00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:36.609
If I buy crude oil for

142
00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:42.623
delivery in a year and the

143
00:14:42.624 --> 00:14:50.825
counterparty has trouble
fulfilling the contract,

144
00:14:50.826 --> 00:14:58.629
other parties, such as an
FCM, stand behind the trade.

145
00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:04.449
So you don't have counterparty
risk in the futures contract if someone

146
00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:11.530
changes their mind or goes
bankrupt. The trade still goes through.

147
00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:16.129
That means there are many checks along

148
00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:21.650
the way to prevent that from happening.

149
00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:27.209
Margin is particularly important.
If it starts running out,

150
00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:32.530
the FCM can close the position
unless you deposit more capital.

151
00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:46.064
FCMs face strict requirements.
Customer funds are reported,

152
00:15:46.065 --> 00:15:51.589
and the authorities oversee this.

153
00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:52.820
The

154
00:15:56.834 --> 00:16:05.109
CFTC sets the regulations,

155
00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:07.670
and the NFA ensures they're followed.

156
00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:21.005
Whether you invest in
Babylon, use a CTA program

157
00:16:21.006 --> 00:16:28.428
or simply have a futures
account in your own name,

158
00:16:28.429 --> 00:16:32.699
the account is segregated.

159
00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:40.071
It's a margin account rather
than a bank account, and it's

160
00:16:40.072 --> 00:16:48.700
kept separate from the
FCM's own funds and assets.

161
00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:51.449
If the FCM

162
00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:55.490
becomes insolvent,

163
00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:07.420
those accounts remain
separate from the company.

164
00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:15.637
FCMs have gone bankrupt in the past,

165
00:17:15.638 --> 00:17:21.959
but people have still received
their deposited funds back.

166
00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.600
And there's no cap on that.

167
00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:36.439
You can't call it guaranteed, but an FCM
is simply not allowed to use those funds.

168
00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:44.920
They're held in two special accounts
monitored by the authorities.

169
00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:07.289
It's a margin account,
holding the collateral you put up.

170
00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:14.424
For us, a $1 million account would

171
00:18:14.425 --> 00:18:20.290
allow us to buy a large amount of oil.

172
00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:26.888
But we'll never reach a situation where we're

173
00:18:26.889 --> 00:18:32.289
asked to deposit more
money to fulfil the contract.

174
00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:37.249
We're not doing this to buy the
physical product, and we only

175
00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:45.520
use a small part of the total capital.

176
00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:52.768
It's only in strong trends,
when things are going very well,

177
00:18:52.769 --> 00:18:58.309
that we really use our margin.

178
00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:01.881
Most of the time, the money is sitting

179
00:19:01.882 --> 00:19:08.489
there without actually being used.

180
00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:16.333
It's different from buying a
share for $10,000 and paying

181
00:19:16.334 --> 00:19:24.310
that amount. Here, the money
is collateral to ensure

182
00:19:24.311 --> 00:19:31.750
the trade can be fulfilled in the future.

183
00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:44.549
This is overseen by the CFTC, the US

184
00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:50.910
equivalent of Sweden's Financial
Supervisory Authority for derivatives.

185
00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:01.794
Then there's the NFA, a self-regulatory

186
00:20:01.795 --> 00:20:08.974
organisation that, put simply,

187
00:20:08.975 --> 00:20:13.550
does the work for the CFTC.

188
00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:26.073
The NFA is a helpful, service-minded

189
00:20:26.074 --> 00:20:32.829
organisation for CTAs, FCMs and brokers,

190
00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:38.770
ensuring that the CFTC's rules are followed.

191
00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:46.262
These checks

192
00:20:46.263 --> 00:20:54.024
are very strict.

193
00:20:54.025 --> 00:21:02.723
They conduct regular audits

194
00:21:02.724 --> 00:21:05.063
of the entire business,

195
00:21:05.064 --> 00:21:13.290
with the frequency depending
on the type of operation.

196
00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:23.016
An FCM may face almost continuous

197
00:21:23.017 --> 00:21:27.390
auditing and oversight.

198
00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:36.409
For a CTA, audits are normally unscheduled,

199
00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:44.089
occurring roughly every three or four years.

200
00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:52.569
Over a two-year period, I had two audits

201
00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:59.686
in succession. They're demanding:
an audit takes four months,

202
00:21:59.687 --> 00:22:06.399
including a week-long visit to the office.

203
00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:15.117
When I lived in Dallas, four
people sat beside me from Monday to

204
00:22:15.118 --> 00:22:20.880
Friday, reviewing bank accounts,
customer accounts and every trade.

205
00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:29.560
They then returned to Chicago and
continued the audit for several more months.

206
00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:37.688
There are ongoing checks,

207
00:22:37.689 --> 00:22:45.700
which give investors a degree of reassurance.

208
00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:50.726
Of course, returns aren't guaranteed. This

209
00:22:50.727 --> 00:22:57.919
doesn't protect against market losses.

210
00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:05.583
What this framework provides
is protection for the

211
00:23:05.584 --> 00:23:14.283
capital. As CTAs, we also have

212
00:23:14.284 --> 00:23:23.020
to submit fingerprints to the
NFA for clearance by the FBI.

213
00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:29.540
That may provide some additional reassurance.

214
00:23:31.270 --> 00:23:38.870
To summarise, investors buy
shares directly in Babylon.

215
00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:48.168
The company holds the capital, and
Hallenborg CTA manages it under

216
00:23:48.169 --> 00:23:53.600
a mandate. There's a disclosure
document for the CTA program we use.

217
00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:58.450
If you're interested, you
can email us for a copy.

218
00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:03.962
GFF Brokers manages the relationship,

219
00:24:03.963 --> 00:24:11.076
and StoneX gives us
access to the futures markets

220
00:24:11.077 --> 00:24:17.710
and guarantees our positions
against the counterparty.

221
00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:26.240
Everything operates under the
CFTC and NFA regulatory framework.

222
00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:37.640
I've

223
00:24:41.752 --> 00:24:48.316
written a short book about Managed Futures

224
00:24:48.317 --> 00:24:50.960
that hasn't been published yet.

225
00:24:51.340 --> 00:24:59.548
It's available as a PDF.
If you'd like to read it

226
00:24:59.549 --> 00:25:05.279
before publication, email
me and I'll send you a copy.

227
00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:13.886
This should be a little more
interesting than the regulations

228
00:25:13.887 --> 00:25:19.820
and structure we've just covered.

229
00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:28.421
It's divided into sections.
The first addresses the

230
00:25:28.422 --> 00:25:34.820
problem with a traditional
portfolio of stocks and bonds.

231
00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:47.183
I explain that investing in Managed

232
00:25:47.184 --> 00:25:55.861
Futures or trend following
doesn't always have to

233
00:25:55.862 --> 00:26:02.039
be speculative, with the
aim of maximising returns.

234
00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:09.000
That's not how most investors
use Managed Futures.

235
00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:17.079
Many people look at the
returns, compare them with the

236
00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:21.799
stock market, and decide
whether to invest on that basis.

237
00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:29.362
The book shows how institutions
and larger investors use this

238
00:26:29.363 --> 00:26:34.511
type of investment to diversify
across market environments,

239
00:26:34.512 --> 00:26:42.919
so their holdings don't all move
together when economic conditions change.

240
00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:50.600
Most portfolios today depend on economic
growth and a rising stock market.

241
00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:58.418
Many CTA programs and Managed Futures
strategies don't have that correlation.

242
00:26:58.419 --> 00:27:02.058
They offer another component that isn't

243
00:27:02.059 --> 00:27:10.640
tied to economic growth or the stock market.

244
00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:18.780
This sector can perform well
when everything else is falling.

245
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:28.487
I explain why trends exist and how they

246
00:27:28.488 --> 00:27:35.271
arise, so you can judge whether
they'll continue. I also

247
00:27:35.272 --> 00:27:43.780
briefly cover the strategies
used in Managed Futures.

248
